<AtlasLayout title="Methodology">
  <article class="page-width content-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">How to read the atlas</p><h1>Mechanisms with context.</h1><p class="hero-description">A relationship is useful only when its meaning, evidence, and limits are clear. The atlas keeps those details close to the map.</p>
    <aside class="preview-notice"><span class="notice-symbol" aria-hidden="true">i</span><div><strong>Milestone 2 scientific candidate</strong><p>The current map has source-linked claims and treatment records, with recorded source checks. Human domain-review approval is pending; the proposed synthesis is not a published scientific reference.</p></div></aside>
    <h2>Read a connection</h2><p>Arrows show directed relationships. A line without arrowheads represents a symmetric association, which does not assert a causal direction. Select a node or line to see the attached claim and its evidence. A feedback loop is shown by distinct connections in both directions.</p>
    <h2>Inspect the evidence</h2><p>Claims carry a disease context, linked evidence items, sources, and limitations. Supporting, qualifying, and contradicting evidence have distinct roles. An empty category means no evidence has been entered in that category; it does not establish that such evidence is absent from the literature.</p>
    <h2 id="scoring">Scores: Not assessed</h2><p>The candidate has no numerical assessments. Every mechanism uses a common baseline size and neutral evidence styling. Position does not imply importance, and zero is never substituted for an unassessed value. Calibrated and approved scoring may be added after the MVP.</p>
    <h2 id="source-checking">Source checking and scientific review</h2><p>Source checked means a primary report or official status document was inspected for the stated claim and location. Most study checks use abstracts; the Sources page records the access scope. This records source traceability, not human approval of the complete mechanism.</p><p>Human genetics, cultured human cells, animal experiments, observational correlations and randomized trials have different implications. The inspector identifies the study system and interpretation. A directed arrow may represent a model-specific mechanism or an explicitly labelled hypothesis; it does not establish that the link causes every case of human SLE.</p><p>The proposed maintaining circuit joins evidence from several systems. Its closure is an editorial synthesis awaiting domain review, not a single experimentally demonstrated human causal loop. The renal branch uses an Fc-receptor knockout mouse experiment. Its immune complexes are not assumed to have the same composition as those used in the pDC culture experiments.</p><h2>Therapeutic interventions</h2><p>Rx counts identify direct-target interventions. Select a treatment to inspect target, mechanism, indication, status, verification date, trial evidence and limitations. An indirect effect is distinguished from target binding. Approval refers to the listed jurisdiction and indication; an experimental drug is not presented as established treatment. Trial success does not determine mechanistic weight.</p>
    <h2 id="publication-policy">Scientific review and publication</h2><p>A curated release needs an explicitly configured domain reviewer and approval of the exact candidate revision and content digest. Changes after approval invalidate that decision. Candidate and under-review scientific records cannot be published.</p><p>Published releases will embed their entities, relationships, claims, evidence, assessments, sources, contexts, and layout. The first release may have a null parent. Later releases must reference an existing immutable parent. A working-record edit cannot change an earlier release.</p><p>The development build is an internal preview, not the scientific publication step. Publication validation must reject this unapproved candidate. The repository review policy explains the configured reviewer and approval requirements.</p>
    <h2>Scientific use</h2><p>Causome is intended for mechanism exploration by scientifically literate users. The initial atlas does not provide patient-specific advice, diagnosis, or treatment recommendations. Source verification and human domain review are required before the MVP launch.</p>
